::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>What does MLA.GEO.RP.1 focus on? {
~Constructing angle bisectors with a compass#That belongs to a construction standard.
~Finding coordinate distances only#Coordinate distance is not the focus of this standard.
~Calculating volume of solids#Volume is not part of this lesson standard.
=Proving and applying relationships involving lines, angles, and angle pairs#Correct. This standard focuses on line and angle reasoning.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>What does MLA.GEO.RP.5 focus on? {
~Using one example to prove every claim#One example is usually not enough to prove a universal claim.
~Measuring angles with a protractor#Measurement is not proof construction.
=Constructing valid geometric proofs, including proofs by contradiction#Correct. This standard focuses on valid proof construction.
~Copying segments and angles#Copying figures belongs to a construction standard.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>What does MLA.GEO.RP.6 focus on? {
~Drawing diagrams that look accurate#Appearance alone is not enough for this standard.
=Evaluating arguments and using counterexamples to disprove false statements#Correct. This standard focuses on validity and counterexamples.
~Memorizing labels without reasoning#Labels alone do not evaluate arguments.
~Finding area from formulas#Area formulas are outside this lesson scope.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If &angle;1 and &angle;2 are vertical angles, which statement is supported? {
=&angle;1 â‰… &angle;2#Correct. Vertical angles are congruent.
~m&angle;1 + m&angle;2 = 180Â°#Vertical angles are congruent; they are not always supplementary.
~&angle;1 and &angle;2 form a linear pair#Vertical angles are opposite angles, not adjacent linear-pair angles.
~m&angle;1 = 90Â°#Vertical angles do not have to be right angles.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If &angle;A and &angle;B form a linear pair, which reason supports m&angle;A + m&angle;B = 180Â°? {
~Vertical angles are congruent#This reason applies to vertical angles, not linear pairs.
~All angles are congruent#This is false.
=Angles that form a linear pair are supplementary#Correct. Supplementary angles have measures that add to 180Â°.
~The angles look like equal angles#Appearance does not prove supplementary relationship.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly uses supplementary angle reasoning? {
~If two angles are supplementary, their measures add to 90Â°#Supplementary angles add to 180Â°.
~If two angles are supplementary, they must be congruent#Supplementary angles are not always congruent.
~If two angles are supplementary, they must be vertical angles#Supplementary angles do not have to be vertical angles.
=If two angles are supplementary, their measures add to 180Â°#Correct. This is the definition of supplementary angles.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>A proof states &angle;3 â‰… &angle;4 because &angle;3 and &angle;4 are vertical angles. What role does because &angle;3 and &angle;4 are vertical angles play? {
=It is evidence used with a known angle relationship#Correct. The vertical-angle relationship supports the conclusion.
~It is the conclusion#The conclusion is that the angles are congruent.
~It is a counterexample#This statement supports the proof; it does not disprove a claim.
~It is an unsupported guess#Vertical-angle information is valid evidence.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is a counterexample? {
~A second example that supports a true claim#A counterexample disproves, not supports.
~A diagram that looks accurate#Appearance is not the defining feature of a counterexample.
~A proof with no reasons#A proof requires reasons.
=One example that shows a statement is false#Correct. A single valid counterexample disproves a universal claim.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which counterexample disproves the claim All rectangles are squares? {
~A square with side length 5#A square is a rectangle and does not disprove the claim.
=A rectangle with side lengths 4 and 7#Correct. It is a rectangle but not a square.
~A triangle with side lengths 4, 4, and 7#A triangle is not a rectangle.
~A circle#A circle is not a rectangle.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which counterexample disproves the claim All acute angles are congruent? {
~Two 60Â° angles#These are congruent, so they do not disprove the claim.
~A 90Â° angle and a 45Â° angle#A 90Â° angle is not acute.
=A 30Â° angle and a 50Â° angle#Correct. Both are acute, but they are not congruent.
~Two right angles#Right angles are not acute angles.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which counterexample disproves the claim All quadrilaterals have four right angles? {
=A parallelogram with no right angles#Correct. It is a quadrilateral without four right angles.
~A rectangle#A rectangle has four right angles, so it supports rather than disproves the claim.
~A square#A square has four right angles, so it does not disprove the claim.
~A right triangle#A triangle is not a quadrilateral.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which argument is valid? {
~The segments are congruent because they look the same length#Appearance alone is not valid evidence.
=The angles are congruent because they are vertical angles#Correct. The conclusion is supported by a known angle relationship.
~The lines are parallel because they look parallel#Appearance alone is not valid evidence.
~The angle is right because it seems close to 90Â°#Approximate appearance is not proof.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>Why is one example not enough to prove a universal claim such as All quadrilaterals are rectangles? {
~One example can only disprove statements, never support thinking#One example can support exploration, but it does not prove a universal claim.
~Universal claims are never true#Some universal claims are true when proven.
=A universal claim must work for every case, not just one case#Correct. Universal claims require reasoning that covers all cases.
~Counterexamples only work with numbers#Counterexamples can use geometric figures too.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>What is the purpose of reasons in a proof? {
~To make the proof longer#Reasons are for justification, not length.
~To replace all given information#Given information is still part of the proof.
~To avoid using mathematical vocabulary#Proof reasons require precise vocabulary.
=To justify why each statement follows#Correct. Reasons connect statements to definitions, properties, or theorems.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair best describes a basic proof setup? {
~Guess and drawing#A proof requires statements and reasons, not guessing.
=Given and prove#Correct. A proof commonly starts with given information and a statement to prove.
~Example and opinion#A proof is based on valid reasoning, not opinion.
~Color and shape#Visual features alone do not form a proof setup.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>What is the first step in a proof by contradiction? {
=Assume the opposite of what you want to prove#Correct. A contradiction proof starts with the negation of the desired conclusion.
~State the conclusion without support#Proofs require support.
~Find one example that looks correct#Contradiction proof does not begin with one visual example.
~Erase the original claim#The original claim remains what the proof is trying to establish.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>In a proof by contradiction, what happens after the opposite assumption leads to an impossible result? {
~The original statement is rejected#An impossible result rejects the opposite assumption.
~The proof becomes a counterexample#A contradiction proof is not the same as giving a counterexample.
~The diagram must be ignored#A diagram may still help, but the reasoning drives the proof.
=The original statement is supported as true#Correct. The contradiction shows the opposite assumption cannot be true.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes a valid geometric proof? {
~A list of conclusions with no reasons#A proof must justify conclusions.
~A drawing that appears correct#Appearance alone is not proof.
=A logical argument with statements supported by reasons#Correct. Valid proofs connect each statement to a reason.
~A single example that matches the claim#A single example does not prove a universal geometric statement.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which reason supports m&angle;A + m&angle;B = 180Â° if &angle;A and &angle;B are supplementary? {
=Definition of supplementary angles#Correct. Supplementary angles have measures that add to 180Â°.
~Vertical angles are congruent#This does not explain supplementary angle measures.
~Definition of midpoint#Midpoint applies to segments, not angle sums.
~The angles look adjacent#Appearance or adjacency alone is not the definition.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>A proof states that two lines are parallel because corresponding angles are congruent. What type of evidence is being used? {
~Appearance-based evidence#The reason is a line-angle relationship, not appearance.
=A line and angle relationship#Correct. Corresponding angle congruence can support a parallel-line conclusion.
~Measurement guess#The reason is based on a relationship, not a guess.
~A counterexample#The statement supports a conclusion; it does not disprove one.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>A student claims all triangles are right triangles. Which counterexample disproves the claim? {
~A right triangle#This supports the claim for one case, but it does not disprove it.
~A triangle with one 90Â° angle#This is a right triangle.
~A rectangle#A rectangle is not a triangle.
=An equilateral triangle#Correct. An equilateral triangle has no right angle, so it disproves the claim.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which proof step is incomplete? {
=Statement: &angle;1 â‰… &angle;2. Reason: It looks true.#Correct. Appearance is not a valid proof reason.
~Statement: &angle;1 and &angle;2 are vertical angles. Reason: Given.#This statement has a valid reason.
~Statement: &angle;1 â‰… &angle;2. Reason: Vertical angles are congruent.#This statement has a valid reason.
~Statement: m&angle;A + m&angle;B = 180Â°. Reason: Definition of supplementary angles.#This statement has a valid reason.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.6<br><br>Question:<br>What is wrong with the argument The segments are congruent because they are drawn about the same length? {
~Congruent segments cannot appear in a diagram#Congruent segments can appear in diagrams.
~Segments never need evidence#Segment congruence requires evidence.
=It uses appearance instead of a valid reason#Correct. A valid argument needs evidence such as markings, measurements, or a proven relationship.
~The conclusion is always false#The conclusion may be true, but the stated reason is not valid.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.1<br><br>Question:<br>If two angles form a linear pair and one angle measures 65Â°, what is the measure of the other angle? {
=115Â°#Correct. Linear-pair angles add to 180Â°, and 180Â° - 65Â° = 115Â°.
~65Â°#Linear-pair angles are supplementary, not necessarily congruent.
~125Â°#This subtracts incorrectly from 190Â° rather than 180Â°.
~25Â°#This does not create a sum of 180Â° with 65Â°.
}

::GEO_U01_L07_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U01_L07_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.RP.5<br><br>Question:<br>Which explanation best supports the conclusion &angle;1 â‰… &angle;2? {
~They appear to have the same measure#Appearance alone is not proof.
=They are vertical angles, and vertical angles are congruent#Correct. This gives evidence and a valid reason.
~They are both drawn with dark lines#Line darkness is not mathematical evidence.
~They are near each other in the diagram#Location alone does not prove congruence.
}

